### What is a functional interface?

A functional interface has exactly one abstract method. It is the type that lambda expressions and method references target. The annotation @FunctionalInterface is optional but strongly recommended because it provides compile-time verification.

### What are the rules for counting abstract methods?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

@FunctionalInterface
interface AccountValidator {
    boolean validate(String accountId, BigDecimal balance); // ONE abstract method

    // Default: has body, does NOT count
    default AccountValidator and(AccountValidator other) {
        return (id, bal) -> this.validate(id, bal) && other.validate(id, bal);
    }

    // Static: has body, does NOT count
    static AccountValidator alwaysValid() {
        return (id, bal) -> true;
    }

    // Object method: does NOT count
    String toString();
}
```

### Why is @FunctionalInterface recommended?

```java
package com.bank.core;

@FunctionalInterface
interface TransferProcessor {
    void process(String fromAccount, String toAccount);
    // void audit(String fromAccount, String toAccount);
    // COMPILE ERROR! @FunctionalInterface with 2 abstract methods!
}
// Without @FunctionalInterface, adding audit() silently breaks
// all lambda usage sites across the codebase.
```

### What are the four core functional interfaces?

| Interface | Signature | Purpose | Banking Example |
|-----------|-----------|---------|----------------|
| Predicate<T> | boolean test(T t) | Filtering | `account -> account.isActive()` |
| Function<T,R> | R apply(T t) | Transformation | `account -> account.getBalance()` |
| Consumer<T> | void accept(T t) | Side effect | `account -> auditLog.save(account)` |
| Supplier<T> | T get() | Factory/lazy creation | `() -> UUID.randomUUID().toString()` |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.*;
import java.util.List;

public class CoreFunctionalDemo {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", "Priya", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", "Amit", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        );

        Predicate<Account> isActive = Account::active;
        Predicate<Account> isLarge = a -> a.balance().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0;
        Function<Account, String> toSummary = a -> a.id() + ": Rs." + a.balance();
        Consumer<Account> logAccount = a -> System.out.println("Processed: " + a.id());
        Supplier<String> txnId = () -> "TXN-" + System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Composition
        accounts.stream()
            .filter(isActive.and(isLarge))
            .map(toSummary)
            .forEach(System.out::println);

        System.out.println("Generated ID: " + txnId.get());
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001: Rs.125000
CUR3001: Rs.250000
Generated ID: TXN-1705312800000
```

### How does composition work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.*;

public class CompositionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Predicate composition
        Predicate<BigDecimal> positive = amt -> amt.signum() > 0;
        Predicate<BigDecimal> large = amt -> amt.compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0;
        Predicate<BigDecimal> positiveLarge = positive.and(large);
        Predicate<BigDecimal> notPositive = positive.negate();

        // Function composition
        Function<String, BigDecimal> parse = BigDecimal::new;
        Function<BigDecimal, String> format = amt -> "Rs." + amt.toPlainString();
        Function<String, String> parseAndFormat = parse.andThen(format);

        System.out.println(parseAndFormat.apply("50000")); // Rs.50000

        // Consumer composition
        Consumer<String> log = msg -> System.out.println("LOG: " + msg);
        Consumer<String> audit = msg -> System.out.println("AUDIT: " + msg);
        Consumer<String> both = log.andThen(audit);

        both.accept("Transfer completed");
    }
}
```

```text
Rs.50000
LOG: Transfer completed
AUDIT: Transfer completed
```

### Can a functional interface extend another interface?

Yes, as long as the combined result still has exactly one abstract method. If the parent has an abstract method, the child must override it as a default method.

```java
package com.bank.core;

interface Describable { String describe(); } // 1 abstract method

// WRONG: 2 abstract methods (execute + describe)
// @FunctionalInterface
// interface AccountAction extends Describable { void execute(); }

// CORRECT: override parent abstract as default
@FunctionalInterface
interface AccountAction extends Describable {
    void execute(); // 1 abstract method
    @Override
    default String describe() { return "Account action"; } // parent becomes default
}
```

### What are the extended family interfaces?

BiFunction<T,U,R> takes two inputs and returns one output. UnaryOperator<T> extends Function where input and output are the same type. BinaryOperator<T> extends BiFunction where all three types are the same, used in reduce operations. BiPredicate<T,U> takes two inputs and returns boolean.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The seven core interfaces (Predicate, Function, Consumer, Supplier, UnaryOperator, BinaryOperator, BiFunction) plus their Bi variants cover all common lambda shapes. Creating custom functional interfaces is appropriate only when the domain name adds clarity, like `AccountValidator` being more readable than `BiPredicate<String, BigDecimal>`. Named predicates stored as static factory methods in a validation rules class are reusable across different streams and independently testable.
